# Model Assertion Reference
Structure and key fields of an Ubuntu Core model assertion.

## What is a model assertion?
A model assertion is a signed declaration by a brand (hardware vendor or OEM)
that defines a specific Ubuntu Core device configuration: which snaps are
seeded, which store to use, and device-specific settings. It is stored in the
ubuntu-seed partition and used by snapd during first boot.

## Minimal example (unsigned, for testing)
```yaml
type: model
authority-id: my-brand
brand-id: my-brand
model: my-device
architecture: amd64
base: core24
grade: dangerous
snaps:
  - name: pc
    type: gadget
    default-channel: 24/stable
  - name: pc-kernel
    type: kernel
    default-channel: 24/stable
  - name: core24
    type: base
    default-channel: latest/stable
  - name: snapd
    type: snapd
    default-channel: latest/stable
timestamp: 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z
```

## Key fields

type               Always "model".
authority-id       The account ID that signs this assertion.
brand-id           The brand's account ID (usually the same as authority-id
                   for self-signed devices).
model              Identifier for this device model. Alphanumeric + hyphens.
architecture       Target CPU architecture: amd64, arm64, armhf, riscv64, etc.
base               The base snap: core22, core24, etc.
grade              Security grade:
                     signed     — all snaps must be signed (production).
                     secured    — all snaps + model itself must be signed.
                     dangerous  — unsigned snaps allowed (development/testing).
timestamp          ISO 8601 timestamp when the assertion was created.

## snaps list fields

name               Snap name as registered in the store.
type               gadget, kernel, base, snapd, or app.
default-channel    Channel to install from (track/risk, e.g. 24/stable).
id                 Snap store ID (optional; used for verification).
presence           optional — snap is seeded only if available in the store;
                   required — default; snap must be present.
modes              List of modes this snap is installed in (run, recover, etc.)

## Store and access control

store              Snap store ID. Omit for the global store. Set to your DSS
                   ID for a Dedicated Snap Store. See: idiot env store status

## Creating a signed assertion
1. Register as a brand on the store.
2. Create the model YAML (without the assertion headers).
3. Sign with snapcraft: snapcraft sign-build <model.yaml>
   (requires store account key)

## Working with models
idiot models pick             Interactive model selector from canonical/models
idiot models update           Update the local models repository
idiot inspect image <img>     Read model from a built disk image

